=========== HOW TO TAKE INPUTS VIA HTML AND STORE IN LOCAL MONGODB ===========

1. ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW:
   HTML Form (Client-Side) 
       ---> HTTP POST (fetch / Form submit) 
       ---> Node.js / Express Server (Backend API) 
       ---> MongoDB (Local Database at mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017)

   * Note: Web browsers cannot directly connect to MongoDB databases for security & network protocol reasons. 
     A lightweight backend server (e.g., Express.js) acts as an intermediary to process, validate, and save data.

2. LOCAL MONGODB SETUP ON YOUR SYSTEM:
   - Check if MongoDB is installed and running:
       mongosh --eval "db.version()"
     or check system service:
       sudo systemctl status mongod
       sudo systemctl start mongod
   - Default connection string for local instance:
       mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/portfolio_db

3. BACKEND SERVER SETUP (Node.js + Express + Mongoose):
   Step 3.1 - Initialize & install packages in backend directory:
       npm install express mongoose cors dotenv multer

   Step 3.2 - Server file (e.g., server.js):
       const express = require('express');
       const mongoose = require('mongoose');
       const cors = require('cors');

       const app = express();
       app.use(cors());
       app.use(express.json());
       app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true }));

       // Connect to local MongoDB instance
       mongoose.connect('mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/portfolio_db')
         .then(() => console.log('Connected to local MongoDB successfully'))
         .catch(err => console.error('MongoDB connection error:', err));

       // Define Schema and Model for Form Input
       const ContactSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
         name: { type: String, required: true },
         phone: { type: String, required: true },
         email: { type: String, required: true },
         reason: { type: String, maxlength: 500 },
         submittedAt: { type: Date, default: Date.now }
       });
       const Contact = mongoose.model('Contact', ContactSchema);

       // POST Endpoint to receive form submission
       app.post('/api/contact', async (req, res) => {
         try {
           const { name, phone, email, reason } = req.body;
           const newEntry = new Contact({ name, phone, email, reason });
           await newEntry.save();
           res.status(201).json({ success: true, message: 'Data saved to MongoDB successfully!' });
         } catch (error) {
           res.status(500).json({ success: false, error: error.message });
         }
       });

       // Review Schema & Model
       const ReviewSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
         name: { type: String, required: true },
         rating: { type: Number, min: 1, max: 5, required: true },
         review: { type: String, required: true },
         phone: String,
         email: String,
         submittedAt: { type: Date, default: Date.now }
       });
       const Review = mongoose.model('Review', ReviewSchema);

       app.post('/api/review', async (req, res) => {
         try {
           const newReview = new Review(req.body);
           await newReview.save();
           res.status(201).json({ success: true, message: 'Review saved to MongoDB successfully!' });
         } catch (error) {
           res.status(500).json({ success: false, error: error.message });
         }
       });

       const PORT = process.env.PORT || 5000;
       app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${PORT}`));

4. HTML FRONTEND IMPLEMENTATION:
   Method A: Using JavaScript fetch() (Recommended - no page reload)
   ------------------------------------------------------------
   <!-- HTML Form -->
   <form id="contactForm">
     <input type="text" id="name" name="name" placeholder="Name" required />
     <input type="tel" id="phone" name="phone" placeholder="Phone" required />
     <input type="email" id="email" name="email" placeholder="Email" required />
     <textarea id="reason" name="reason" maxlength="500" placeholder="Reason to connect"></textarea>
     <button type="submit">Submit</button>
   </form>

   <script>
     document.getElementById('contactForm').addEventListener('submit', async function(e) {
       e.preventDefault();

       const formData = {
         name: document.getElementById('name').value,
         phone: document.getElementById('phone').value,
         email: document.getElementById('email').value,
         reason: document.getElementById('reason').value
       };

       try {
         const response = await fetch('http://localhost:5000/api/contact', {
           method: 'POST',
           headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
           body: JSON.stringify(formData)
         });

         const result = await response.json();
         if (result.success) {
           alert('Submitted successfully!');
           document.getElementById('contactForm').reset();
         } else {
           alert('Error: ' + result.error);
         }
       } catch (err) {
         console.error('Fetch error:', err);
         alert('Failed to connect to backend server');
       }
     });
   </script>

   Method B: Direct HTML Form Action POST (Traditional form submission)
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   <form action="http://localhost:5000/api/contact" method="POST">
     <input type="text" name="name" required />
     <input type="tel" name="phone" required />
     <input type="email" name="email" required />
     <textarea name="reason" maxlength="500"></textarea>
     <button type="submit">Submit</button>
   </form>

5. HANDLING FILE ATTACHMENTS (FOR PDF / JPG / ETC.):
   If the form contains attachments, use multipart/form-data with multer:
   - Frontend:
     const form = document.getElementById('contactForm');
     const formData = new FormData(form);
     fetch('http://localhost:5000/api/contact', { method: 'POST', body: formData });
   - Backend:
     const upload = multer({ dest: 'uploads/' });
     app.post('/api/contact', upload.single('attachment'), async (req, res) => {
       const filePath = req.file ? req.file.path : null;
       const newEntry = new Contact({ ...req.body, attachmentPath: filePath });
       await newEntry.save();
       res.json({ success: true });
     });

